{% macro multiple_annotators(examples, legends, marker, legacy_aliases=false) -%}
{% if marker %}
{% if legacy_aliases %}
{{ anchor("pool-based-al-strategies-for-mulitple-annotators") }}

.. _pool-based-active-learning-multi-annotator-learning:

{% endif %}
{{ heading("Single-output Classification with Multiple Annotators", marker) }}

{% endif %}
Each sample has one class label to predict. Several annotators can label it,
and the strategy selects sample–annotator pairs. The upper plot shows the
mean utility over the annotators, the decision boundary, the majority vote of
each labeled sample, and the selected sample. The middle plots show the labels requested from each
annotator and compare its true accuracy with its estimate. The lower plot
shows the test accuracy of this run.

{{ legend(legends["pool-multi-annotator"]) }}

{{ gallery(examples["pool-multi-annotator"]) }}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro pool_classification(examples, legends, marker, legacy_aliases=false) -%}
{% if legacy_aliases %}
{{ anchor("pool-based-al-strategies") }}

.. _pool-based-active-learning-classification:

{% endif %}
{{ heading("Single-output Classification", marker) }}

One annotator gives one class label per selected sample, and each cycle
acquires one sample. The upper plot shows the utilities, the classifier's
decision boundary, and the selected sample. The lower plot shows the test
accuracy of this run.

{{ legend(legends["pool-classification"]) }}

{{ gallery(examples["pool-classification"]) }}

{{ heading("Single-output Classification with Batch Queries", marker) }}

Each cycle acquires four samples. Panel *i* shows the utilities for the
*i*-th selection in the batch and marks the earlier selections of the batch.
All panels use the same classifier and labels. The lower plot shows the test
accuracy of this run.

{{ legend(legends["pool-classification-batch"]) }}

{{ gallery(examples["pool-classification-batch"]) }}

{{ heading("Multi-label Classification", marker) }}

One annotator gives a complete vector of binary labels for each selected
sample. These examples acquire one vector per cycle. The three label panels
share the utilities and show a separate decision boundary for each label. The
fourth panel shows the exact-match accuracy of this run, the share of test
samples whose complete label vector is predicted correctly.

{{ legend(legends["pool-multilabel"]) }}

{{ gallery(examples["pool-multilabel"]) }}

{{ multiple_annotators(examples, legends, marker, legacy_aliases) }}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro pool_regression(examples, legends) -%}
One annotator gives a continuous label for each selected sample. The upper
plot shows the samples, the true function, and the prediction, with its
standard deviation where the regressor provides one. The middle plot shows
the utilities and marks the selected samples. The lower plot shows the test
R² of this run. Examples acquire one sample per cycle unless their caption
names a batch.

{{ legend(legends["pool-regression"]) }}

{{ gallery(examples["pool-regression"]) }}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro stream_classification(examples, legends) -%}
One annotator gives one class label per queried sample. Samples arrive in
sequence, and the strategy decides within its budget whether to request each
sample's label. The animation shows arriving samples and the evolving
decision boundary.

{{ legend(legends["stream-classification"]) }}

{{ gallery(examples["stream-classification"]) }}
{%- endmacro %}
